So here postmodernism steps in and importantly,

00:15:19.752 --> 00:15:22.129
instead of returning to traditional narratives,

00:15:22.129 --> 00:15:24.840
starts to question the value of narrative itself.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:28.385
Postmodernism is saying, maybe this idea that we

00:15:28.385 --> 00:15:32.973
can come to an understanding of the objective truth using science and

00:15:32.973 --> 00:15:36.477
reason is itself a narrative like

00:15:36.477 --> 00:15:41.315
the narratives of old in tradition that modernism rejected.

00:15:41.315 --> 00:15:43.901
Postmodernism is also looking at things like the rise of

00:15:43.901 --> 00:15:47.029
fascism in the 20th century and understanding how

00:15:47.029 --> 00:15:49.865
the narrative surrounding that are part of what made

00:15:49.865 --> 00:15:52.868
it a powerful force for evil in the 20th century.

00:15:52.868 --> 00:15:55.537
Postmodernism in broad strokes is really critiquing

00:15:55.537 --> 00:15:58.791
modernism by critiquing narrative itself,

00:15:58.791 --> 00:16:01.251
saying maybe we should be skeptical of

00:16:01.251 --> 00:16:06.131
any broad overarching narrative that thinks that can explain the world.

00:16:06.131 --> 00:16:08.634
When you start to question the value of narrative

00:16:08.634 --> 00:16:11.553
itself in what is essentially a narrative form,

00:16:11.553 --> 00:16:14.098
things understandably start to get a little weird.

00:16:14.098 --> 00:16:17.601
As we move towards the end of the 20th century in film history,

00:16:17.601 --> 00:16:19.478
we start to see more and more movies

00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:23.107
that don't just question and deconstruct modernist values,

00:16:23.107 --> 00:16:29.154
but which also start to deconstruct the way stories are told in film.

00:16:29.154 --> 00:16:31.448
I thought you said you are a writer.

00:16:31.448 --> 00:16:34.410
This postmodern skepticism of narrative starts to

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:38.038
show up in deconstructions of modernist film-making,

00:16:38.038 --> 00:16:41.500
many of which draw attention to the act of storytelling.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.461
Time becomes fragmented in movies like Pulp Fiction,

00:16:44.461 --> 00:16:46.046
forth walls are broken,

00:16:46.046 --> 00:16:47.923
stories become more self-aware.

00:16:47.923 --> 00:16:51.885
Sincere storytelling gives way to irony, pastiche,

00:16:51.885 --> 00:16:54.346
surrealism and self-reflexivity,

00:16:54.346 --> 00:16:57.224
genre conventions are deconstructed.

00:16:57.224 --> 00:17:01.270
If modernist films were trying to create an illusion of reality so that you,

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:06.025
the viewer, could suspend your disbelief and become fully engrossed in the film,

00:17:06.025 --> 00:17:08.694
some post-modernists films were trying to remind

00:17:08.694 --> 00:17:12.448
the viewer that they were a viewer and that everything they were seeing

00:17:12.448 --> 00:17:16.368
was a story and that stories are constructed and that

00:17:16.368 --> 00:17:21.665
the director and actors are the ones controlling the narrative.

00:17:21.665 --> 00:17:24.752
I think another great example of a post-modernist film

00:17:24.752 --> 00:17:27.463
is Monty Python's quest for the Holy Grail,

00:17:27.463 --> 00:17:30.883
where everything is just one big joke.

00:17:30.883 --> 00:17:36.305
By the end, even the film itself is treated as a big joke.

00:17:36.305 --> 00:17:39.683
The story never gets resolved because why take it seriously?

00:17:39.683 --> 00:17:41.101
Why take anything seriously?

00:17:41.101 --> 00:17:43.353
The characters aren't real, the place isn't real.

00:17:43.353 --> 00:17:44.563
This isn't even real.

00:17:44.563 --> 00:17:45.939
But I think postmodernism,

00:17:45.939 --> 00:17:47.399
especially in narrative forms,

00:17:47.399 --> 00:17:49.193
eventually runs its course.

00:17:49.193 --> 00:17:54.907
You can only deconstruct things for so long before everything turns into chaos.

00:17:54.907 --> 00:17:59.453
You can only subvert expectations so many times before the new expectation becomes

00:17:59.453 --> 00:18:04.750
that expectations will be subverted and it all starts to get a little bit old.

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:09.838
Irony and cynicism are hard to sustain they eventually get tiring.

00:18:09.838 --> 00:18:13.550
While postmodernism might have some very valid critiques to level against

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:18.222
modernism showing how it created a bunch of problems in the 20th century,

00:18:18.222 --> 00:18:23.227
postmodernism itself didn't do anything to really solve

00:18:23.227 --> 00:18:26.188
those problems nor has it prevented or

00:18:26.188 --> 00:18:29.733
solved all the new problems we have in the 21st century.

00:18:29.733 --> 00:18:32.444
As our fears of increased polarization,

00:18:32.444 --> 00:18:36.365
late-stage capitalism and climate change are added on top of

00:18:36.365 --> 00:18:38.992
pre-existing fears from the 20th century

00:18:38.992 --> 00:18:41.787
like nuclear annihilation that never really went away,

00:18:41.787 --> 00:18:46.750
I think people start to long for a modernist optimism again,

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:49.128
that maybe we can actually figure out a way

00:18:49.128 --> 00:18:51.880
to solve some of these problems and make things better.

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:56.468
Postmodern deconstruction can be helpful for a while but it can also give you

00:18:56.468 --> 00:18:59.680
a cynicism that tears everything down

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:03.350
and leaves you unable to sincerely engage with anything.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:04.852
People want meaning.

00:19:04.852 --> 00:19:09.648
Deconstructing all the ways we find narrative meaningful doesn't suddenly

00:19:09.648 --> 00:19:14.736
remove our desire for it or make the comfort it provides disappear.

00:19:14.736 --> 00:19:16.655
People still want to go see a movie.

00:19:16.655 --> 00:19:20.325
People still want to tell stories and listen to stories.

00:19:20.325 --> 00:19:23.245
What comes after postmodernism?

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:31.712
Now it's time for what I know you've all been waiting for.

00:19:31.712 --> 00:19:35.591
By the time we get to 2022 with a movie like Everything Everywhere All at Once,

00:19:35.591 --> 00:19:39.052
many of the elements of postmodernism still remain.

00:19:39.052 --> 00:19:42.890
The movie is a never-ending pastiche of pop culture and

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:46.977
film references much in the same way the postmodern Pulp Fiction was.

00:19:46.977 --> 00:19:50.355
Everything Everywhere constantly plays on genre tropes and has

00:19:50.355 --> 00:19:55.194
a silliness that rivals a film like Monty Python and the Holy Grail all

00:19:55.194 --> 00:19:57.529
while leaning heavily into

00:19:57.529 --> 00:20:00.032
anihilist postmodern deconstruction of

00:20:00.032 --> 00:20:04.077
modernist philosophy like we see in something like No Country for Old Men.

00:20:04.077 --> 00:20:07.873
Right is a tiny box invented by people who are afraid.

00:20:07.873 --> 00:20:09.875
But unlike the postmodern films,

00:20:09.875 --> 00:20:12.002
Everything Everywhere All at Once also contains

00:20:12.002 --> 00:20:16.298
a sincere depiction of sentiment that most postmodern movies never have.

00:20:16.298 --> 00:20:20.427
It also harnesses optimism and emotional sincerity

00:20:20.427 --> 00:20:24.932
that rivals that of modernist films like High Noon or Top Gun: Maverick.

00:20:24.932 --> 00:20:32.147
Metamodernism in general can be thought of as a response to postmodernism.

00:20:32.147 --> 00:20:34.816
It builds on postmodernism.

00:20:34.816 --> 00:20:37.569
It's not just an opposition to postmodernism.

00:20:37.569 --> 00:20:40.155
In Everything Everywhere All at Once,

00:20:40.155 --> 00:20:43.200
nihilism is a big theme.

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:46.328
But if it were merely a postmodern film it would stop there.

00:20:46.328 --> 00:20:50.791
But instead in one of the culminating scenes, Evelyn, the mother,

00:20:50.791 --> 00:20:57.256
she and her daughter reconcile by accepting the nihilism.

00:20:57.256 --> 00:21:02.219
Then I will cherish these few specs of time.

00:21:02.678 --> 00:21:08.433
Life means nothing and therefore it means everything instead of just life means nothing.

00:21:08.433 --> 00:21:12.604
It's a metamodern update of a postmodern insight.

00:21:12.604 --> 00:21:17.985
One of the ways metamodern media response to postmodernism and modernism is by swinging

00:21:17.985 --> 00:21:23.615
wildly back-and-forth between modernist sincerity and postmodern deconstruction.

00:21:23.615 --> 00:21:29.079
This is what a lot of metamodern commentators call oscillation.

00:21:29.079 --> 00:21:30.747
There's more to matter modernism than this,

00:21:30.747 --> 00:21:33.834
but this is one of the easy ways to identify it.

00:21:33.834 --> 00:21:36.920
What this means practically is that a lot of metamodern films

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:40.340
still use or contain the techniques of postmodernist film.

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:43.510
An example of this is the self-aware meta elements in

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:46.555
the movies that I highlighted at the beginning of this video.

00:21:46.555 --> 00:21:51.727
This meta self-awareness is what academics and media critics often call self-reflexivity.

00:21:51.727 --> 00:21:55.856
This self-reflexivity was originally used by a lot of post-modern films,

00:21:55.856 --> 00:21:59.443
but it's still used by metamodern films.

00:21:59.443 --> 00:22:04.323
How do we tell the difference between postmodern and metamodern metaness?

00:22:04.323 --> 00:22:07.576
In postmodernism, this meta self-awareness is usually

00:22:07.576 --> 00:22:11.204
used to draw attention to the limitation,

00:22:11.204 --> 00:22:16.418
structure, boundaries or subjectivity of the work itself. For example.

00:22:16.418 --> 00:22:17.461
What are you doing?

00:22:17.461 --> 00:22:19.921
I'm making a video about metamodernism.

00:22:19.921 --> 00:22:22.591
I'm right in the middle of explaining how self-reflexivity

00:22:22.591 --> 00:22:25.969
is used to call attention to the context of a work.

00:22:25.969 --> 00:22:27.846
Yeah, but why?

00:22:27.846 --> 00:22:30.265
Isn't this just a bunch of academic over assessment

00:22:30.265 --> 00:22:32.476
of artists self obsessing about their own work?

00:22:32.476 --> 00:22:35.729
Don't people just want to enjoy their entertainment in peace?

00:22:35.729 --> 00:22:39.524
That's a good question. I think a big part of why we see this kind

00:22:39.524 --> 00:22:43.153
of self-reflexivity is that viewers

00:22:43.153 --> 00:22:51.036
and artists feel self-conscious about art that is just passive entertainment.

00:22:51.036 --> 00:22:53.705
There's a lot of concern about things that are happening in the world,

00:22:53.705 --> 00:22:56.625
things we see in the news every day and

00:22:56.625 --> 00:22:59.795
there might be a feeling that maybe we should do something about that.

00:22:59.795 --> 00:23:06.301
If you are involved in creating a multi-million dollar art project for entertainment,

00:23:06.301 --> 00:23:08.387
you might be asking yourself,

00:23:08.387 --> 00:23:11.014
should I be doing something better with my time?

00:23:11.014 --> 00:23:12.766
Exactly. Self obsession.

00:23:12.766 --> 00:23:15.936
Well, I think that self-consciousness also extends to the viewer.

00:23:15.936 --> 00:23:18.105
Postmodern self-reflexivity is drawing

00:23:18.105 --> 00:23:20.774
the viewer's attention to the fact that what you're

00:23:20.774 --> 00:23:25.695
seeing might feel really compelling but ultimately it's flawed and subjective.

00:23:25.695 --> 00:23:29.574
Like me breaking the fourth wall to inject a counter-argument?

00:23:29.574 --> 00:23:32.327
Sure. One interpretation of this moment could be that

00:23:32.327 --> 00:23:35.664
it's deconstructing the video essay format by reminding the viewer

00:23:35.664 --> 00:23:39.084
that I'm just a guy who's reading what I've

00:23:39.084 --> 00:23:43.088
written in a script and I have my doubts about what I'm saying,

00:23:43.088 --> 00:23:46.216
which I'm representing in you as my double.

00:23:46.216 --> 00:23:50.887
Then what's the difference between postmodern and meta-modern self-reflexivity?

00:23:50.887 --> 00:23:54.766
We've already mentioned how a sketch like this is a great example of

00:23:54.766 --> 00:23:58.603
postmodern self-reflexivity by deconstructing the video essay.

00:23:58.603 --> 00:24:01.106
Yeah, but you're not using this metal layer

00:24:01.106 --> 00:24:04.109
just to deconstruct the video essay or yourself,

00:24:04.109 --> 00:24:05.944
you're actually using it to construct

00:24:05.944 --> 00:24:09.406
a more effective argument for what you're trying to say.

00:24:09.406 --> 00:24:13.577
I'm affirming what I'm doing is worthwhile if nothing else for me because I

00:24:13.577 --> 00:24:17.664
like this deconstruction and examination of art.

00:24:17.664 --> 00:24:20.584
I think I get it. Metamodernism is

00:24:20.584 --> 00:24:23.712
just postmodernism but more optimistic and less cynical.

00:24:23.712 --> 00:24:26.798
That would be probably a bit of an oversimplification,

00:24:26.798 --> 00:24:29.050
but you're definitely on the right track.

00:24:29.050 --> 00:24:35.056
What we just did was we went from the video essay format to this,

00:24:35.056 --> 00:24:38.602
which is a post-modern deconstructive,

00:24:38.602 --> 00:24:41.396
self-reflexive meta sketch,

00:24:41.396 --> 00:24:47.444
and then we oscillated back into a more modernist optimism

00:24:47.444 --> 00:24:50.489
that tries to find value in this video

00:24:50.489 --> 00:24:54.117
by grounding that value in my own personal experience.

00:24:54.117 --> 00:24:57.454
While not ignoring the flaws and critiques that you've acknowledged.

00:24:57.454 --> 00:25:00.624
While still holding onto an awareness of the limitations.

00:25:00.624 --> 00:25:06.880
I think it's true that a lot of the tropes or aesthetic methods that

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:11.176
metal modernism employees have the exact equivalent and

00:25:11.176 --> 00:25:17.057
postmodernism and the differences in what they're saying with it.

00:25:17.057 --> 00:25:19.893
The 1996 Coen Brothers movie,

00:25:19.893 --> 00:25:21.686
Fargo opens with this text.

00:25:21.686 --> 00:25:23.063
This is a true story.

00:25:23.063 --> 00:25:27.734
The events depicted in this film took place in Minnesota in 1987.

00:25:27.734 --> 00:25:30.737
At the request of the survivors the names have been changed.

00:25:30.737 --> 00:25:32.155
Out of respect for the dead,

00:25:32.155 --> 00:25:35.158
the rest has been told exactly as it occurs,

00:25:35.158 --> 00:25:39.538
except the Coen brothers eventually admitted that nothing about the story was true.

00:25:39.538 --> 00:25:41.331
Joe Coen saying in an interview,

00:25:41.331 --> 00:25:43.875
the only thing true about it is that it's a story.

00:25:43.875 --> 00:25:45.919
To me, this is a post-modern framing device,

00:25:45.919 --> 00:25:48.338
one that is deconstructing the way that

00:25:48.338 --> 00:25:51.883
narrative films claimed to be based on true stories,

00:25:51.883 --> 00:25:56.179
is playing on that trope in some sense to provoke us into considering the fact

00:25:56.179 --> 00:26:01.268
that even movies that claim to be based on true stories are often largely a fabrication.

00:26:01.268 --> 00:26:06.064
Now compare this to the opening statement for Donald Glover's recent show, Swarm.

00:26:06.064 --> 00:26:08.275
Any similarity to actual persons,

00:26:08.275 --> 00:26:11.987
living or dead or actual events is intentional.

00:26:11.987 --> 00:26:15.282
To me, this is a metamodern twist on the same framing device.

00:26:15.282 --> 00:26:17.534
It's saying, sure, this is fictional,

00:26:17.534 --> 00:26:19.661
but within that fiction, there's truth.

00:26:19.661 --> 00:26:22.831
Metamodernism began to emerge in the '90s and

00:26:22.831 --> 00:26:26.418
in film began to really take form throughout the 2000s.

00:26:26.418 --> 00:26:29.754
One of the key early figures in movies is Wes Anderson,

00:26:29.754 --> 00:26:33.091
who really showcases how pastiche reference and

00:26:33.091 --> 00:26:37.512
metanarrative framing devices can be used in a metamodernist way.

00:26:37.512 --> 00:26:41.641
Wes is using all of these things to call attention to the act of storytelling,

00:26:41.641 --> 00:26:43.602
but not to deconstruct story.

00:26:43.602 --> 00:26:48.481
Instead, he uses it to revel in the value of stories themselves,

00:26:48.481 --> 00:26:53.528
using those stories as an avenue to communicate heartfelt emotions.

00:26:53.528 --> 00:26:56.573
I think we can see how some directors who

00:26:56.573 --> 00:27:00.160
started out doing very post-modernist work like Tarantino,

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:02.454
had become more meta modern.

00:27:02.454 --> 00:27:05.165
If Paul fiction subverted audience expectations by

00:27:05.165 --> 00:27:08.668
deconstructing the Hollywood ending Once Upon a Time in Hollywood,

00:27:08.668 --> 00:27:13.006
I think one of the best examples of metamodernism and film recently subverts

00:27:13.006 --> 00:27:17.927
expectations by deconstructing a true story to give us a Hollywood ending.

00:27:17.927 --> 00:27:22.057
I think we're starting to see metamodernism all over film and TV now,

00:27:22.057 --> 00:27:26.102
not just in media that is explicitly self-aware or self-referential,

00:27:26.102 --> 00:27:29.397
but also in genre deconstructing shows like succession,

00:27:29.397 --> 00:27:32.067
Atlanta, Barry, more recently beef,

00:27:32.067 --> 00:27:34.986
and even in reality TV shows like Jury Duty,

00:27:34.986 --> 00:27:37.489
all of which keenly balanced both comedy and

00:27:37.489 --> 00:27:43.495
a erotic detachment with truly sincere, heartfelt, dramatic sentiment.

00:27:43.495 --> 00:27:46.623
You might wonder why culture and art have moved

00:27:46.623 --> 00:27:50.251
into metamodernism as a reaction to postmodernism.

00:27:50.251 --> 00:27:53.672
Instead of just returning to modernism or tradition.

00:27:53.672 --> 00:27:58.760
There are some attempts to just return to those as with Top Gun Maverick,

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:01.680
but I think those are the exceptions rather than the norm.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:03.640
From an artistic filmmaking perspective,

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:09.396
we can't so easily undo postmodernism impact on what is considered to be a normal,

00:28:09.396 --> 00:28:11.439
entertaining way to tell a story now.

00:28:11.439 --> 00:28:13.358
Greg pointed out how many of

00:28:13.358 --> 00:28:16.528
the postmodern elements of storytelling are in and of themselves.

00:28:16.528 --> 00:28:19.906
pretty fun. But that this postmodern art often

00:28:19.906 --> 00:28:23.660
just didn't leave very much room for personal meaning in the midst of that.

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:26.996
When you get into metamodern forms including film,

00:28:26.996 --> 00:28:32.293
you can't just go backwards and forget about all that stuff,

00:28:32.293 --> 00:28:35.505
or viewers will think, you don't get it.

00:28:35.505 --> 00:28:39.008
Viewers will think, you're cheesy or corny.

00:28:39.008 --> 00:28:46.808
Metamodern stuff has a way of working all of the fun postmodern games in,

00:28:46.808 --> 00:28:52.564
but doing it in such a way that it preserves a sense of interiority,

00:28:52.564 --> 00:28:57.736
of personal interiority or what I often call it felt experience.

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:03.283
But another reason that Greg proposed for why metamodernism is

00:29:03.283 --> 00:29:07.912
developing that I agree with is that it isn't just a response to postmodernism.

00:29:07.912 --> 00:29:13.626
It's also simultaneously a response to something we could call hypermodernism.

00:29:13.626 --> 00:29:14.878
You have modernity,

00:29:14.878 --> 00:29:17.797
then post modernism comes along.

00:29:17.797 --> 00:29:20.925
Modernity doesn't just stop existing.

00:29:20.925 --> 00:29:24.304
It's a kind of continues as a thread that

00:29:24.304 --> 00:29:28.308
now is still this thing that we would describe as hypermodernity.

00:29:28.308 --> 00:29:30.810
Yeah. Everything, everywhere all at once.

00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:35.607
It's whole metaphor of the multiple realities.

00:29:35.607 --> 00:29:40.945
I think as a metaphor for the way life in the real-world often feels like

00:29:40.945 --> 00:29:46.034
we're in multiple realities so the insane pace

00:29:46.034 --> 00:29:48.870
of change and of

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:51.790
narrative switching and flipping and points of

00:29:51.790 --> 00:29:54.250
view and to be able to be aware of the points of

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:57.295
view of people from all over the world and people who have

00:29:57.295 --> 00:30:01.090
diametrically opposed political views to you all coming at

00:30:01.090 --> 00:30:04.427
you is something that we have to deal with very much now and it

00:30:04.427 --> 00:30:09.307
threatens to pull apart your sense of self.

00:30:09.307 --> 00:30:12.477
Now we make art that's not just protecting

00:30:12.477 --> 00:30:17.023
the self from other art that was undermining the cell,

00:30:17.023 --> 00:30:20.527
but we make art that protects the sense of self from

00:30:20.527 --> 00:30:25.490
the world that we live in moment-to-moment as we just go about our lives.

00:30:25.490 --> 00:30:29.869
Whatever modernity was has continued in some senses intensified,

00:30:29.869 --> 00:30:33.081
you have people who are trying to deconstruct those things.

00:30:33.081 --> 00:30:37.877
They continue in this other trajectory,

00:30:37.877 --> 00:30:41.422
but they don't necessarily negate each other.

00:30:41.422 --> 00:30:43.925
One doesn't get to win culturally.

00:30:43.925 --> 00:30:48.012
All of the traditional stuff is still right there.

00:30:48.012 --> 00:30:48.972
Right [inaudible] is still as well.

00:30:48.972 --> 00:30:52.684
It still have the traditional stuff we would die like we like.

00:30:52.684 --> 00:30:55.812
[inaudible] That's just fabric,

00:30:55.812 --> 00:30:57.605
that's right, everything together.

00:30:57.605 --> 00:31:01.693
The hyper-modern world is one where because of the Internet and social media,

00:31:01.693 --> 00:31:04.362
were constantly inundated with traditional,

00:31:04.362 --> 00:31:07.740
modernist and post-modernist narratives all at once.

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:12.370
The digital citizen has a perspective of the world that was never before possible.

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:13.788
This is something I've talked about in the video I

00:31:13.788 --> 00:31:15.373
made about hyper-modernism and everything,

00:31:15.373 --> 00:31:17.083
everywhere all at once last year.

00:31:17.083 --> 00:31:21.462
Meta- modernism, I think is an attempt to make sense of this new perspective.

00:31:21.462 --> 00:31:25.008
To pick and choose positive elements from each of

00:31:25.008 --> 00:31:30.889
these old philosophies and to recognize the multiverse that exists around us,

00:31:30.889 --> 00:31:33.266
while still trying to stay grounded in

00:31:33.266 --> 00:31:36.895
the reality of what is truly meaningful in front of you.

00:31:36.895 --> 00:31:38.730
Unlike No Country for Old Men,

00:31:38.730 --> 00:31:41.733
which deconstructs the Western genre, everything,

00:31:41.733 --> 00:31:46.529
everywhere all at once doesn't deconstruct a genre it transcends genre.

00:31:46.529 --> 00:31:49.157
It understands that in the hyper-modern world,

00:31:49.157 --> 00:31:53.119
genre isn't a box that you either have to fit into or deconstruct.

00:31:53.119 --> 00:31:58.291
Now, it is just another tool in the storytellers toolbox.

00:31:58.791 --> 00:32:00.627
You write out.

00:32:00.627 --> 00:32:02.545
We can really see how this plays out in

00:32:02.545 --> 00:32:05.965
the Western sequences from Once Upon a Time in Hollywood,

00:32:05.965 --> 00:32:11.346
or Tarantino presents the act of filming a Western in the style of a Western.

00:32:11.346 --> 00:32:13.890
If No Country for Old Men is deconstructing

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:16.935
the Western genre conventions that we find in high noon.

00:32:16.935 --> 00:32:19.187
Then Once Upon a Time in Hollywood is

00:32:19.187 --> 00:32:22.607
simultaneously deconstructing and drawing our attention to

00:32:22.607 --> 00:32:25.485
those conventions while reinforcing

00:32:25.485 --> 00:32:29.906
their inherent value and using them to entertain the viewer.

00:32:29.906 --> 00:32:31.366
Whatever metal modernism is,

00:32:31.366 --> 00:32:32.575
we're still in the midst of it.

00:32:32.575 --> 00:32:34.827
It's still emerging and being defined.

00:32:34.827 --> 00:32:38.623
There's at least one interview of Daniel Kwan,

00:32:38.623 --> 00:32:41.709
one of the two Daniel's who created everything,

00:32:41.709 --> 00:32:44.629
everywhere all at once where the writer who's

00:32:44.629 --> 00:32:49.258
interviewing him tries to describe it as post-modern.

00:32:49.258 --> 00:32:55.014
They're talking about the way it references a bunch of other fillings.

00:32:55.014 --> 00:32:58.851
Daniel Kwan gently correct her and says,

00:32:58.851 --> 00:33:01.604
well, it's not so much post-modernists.

00:33:01.604 --> 00:33:03.731
I would say meta-modern.

00:33:03.731 --> 00:33:05.942
He explains what he means by that.

00:33:05.942 --> 00:33:10.279
He says something along the lines of that their references are in there

00:33:10.279 --> 00:33:17.912
to say this is actually the world that we all live in like we in our lives.

00:33:17.912 --> 00:33:23.876
We now live in a world where we're inundated with narratives

00:33:23.876 --> 00:33:30.258
coming from all over the place and we feel through those narratives and so everything,

00:33:30.258 --> 00:33:31.676
everywhere all at once.

00:33:31.676 --> 00:33:36.180
If you catch a reference to something like rat tattoo or whatever,

00:33:36.180 --> 00:33:40.977
you don't just stop at the charcoal on the wink and a nod.

00:33:41.602 --> 00:33:44.147
I don't know how to really describe,

00:33:44.147 --> 00:33:48.276
but it brings in a feeling of connection.

00:33:48.276 --> 00:33:50.778
Metamodernism itself isn't good or bad.

00:33:50.778 --> 00:33:53.573
It's not a style or a technique or even something I

00:33:53.573 --> 00:33:57.368
necessarily think artists should consciously try to apply to their work.

00:33:57.368 --> 00:34:01.748
It's just a reflection of changes in cultural attitude, feeling,

00:34:01.748 --> 00:34:06.294
and philosophy that I think naturally start to appear in our storytelling.

00:34:06.294 --> 00:34:11.716
Some meta-modernist stuff to me feels like it's overly concerned with its own self-doubt.

00:34:11.716 --> 00:34:15.386
Sometimes I think storytellers should just get on with it tell the story,

00:34:15.386 --> 00:34:20.683
and stop worrying about whether storytelling is meaningful in some ultimate sense.

00:34:20.683 --> 00:34:26.481
It's absolutely not necessary for somebody to be aware of meta-modernism as

00:34:26.481 --> 00:34:32.403
a trend they can enjoy and appreciate everything just on its own terms and they should.

00:34:32.403 --> 00:34:35.656
That's mainly what I hope people would be doing.

00:34:35.656 --> 00:34:39.410
But for the people who think in this way,

00:34:39.410 --> 00:34:44.082
I feel like it creates a story about the stories.

00:34:44.082 --> 00:34:50.213
You then can appreciate any particular individual artwork as its own thing,

00:34:50.213 --> 00:34:58.638
but you can then also appreciate it as fitting into this larger quilt that's going on.

00:34:58.638 --> 00:34:59.931
There is a thing there.

00:34:59.931 --> 00:35:02.934
I don't think like we're just making it up.

00:35:02.934 --> 00:35:08.147
Then being able to tune into that and tap into that,

00:35:08.147 --> 00:35:12.235
I think just adds another layer of richness to our experience.

00:35:12.235 --> 00:35:13.736
Metamodernism, I think,

00:35:13.736 --> 00:35:16.906
brings back in appreciation for storytelling for

00:35:16.906 --> 00:35:20.576
just its own sake because it's something we enjoy.

00:35:20.576 --> 00:35:23.204
If post-modernism breaks the fourth wall in order to take

00:35:23.204 --> 00:35:25.873
you out of the story then metamodernism breaks

00:35:25.873 --> 00:35:33.339
the fourth wall to open the story up and invite you in because we love a good story.

